          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does acid reflux tend to happen after meals?",
          "answer": "Acid reflux is often worse after meals for a few reasons, according to the passages I retrieved.\n\n- **Stomach filling.** Large or late meals are listed as contributors to reflux (General clinical reference, *Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)*). A fuller stomach puts more pressure on the valve between the stomach and the food pipe, and that valve can let acid back up.\n- **Slow emptying.** Fatty meals slow how fast the stomach empties. The meal stays longer, so reflux has more time to happen. The passage notes that a heavy late dinner is a common trigger for this reason (Gastroenterology educational summary, *Eating with reflux: meal size, timing, and finding individual triggers*).\n- **Specific items.** Alcohol relaxes the valve and increases acid exposure. Carbonated drinks add gas that stretches the stomach (same source).\n- **Position.** Symptoms are also often worse when bending forward or lying down (*Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)*).\n\nThe passages did not explain the exact mechanism in each person, and they did not say which triggers apply to any particular person. They say the best way to find personal triggers is to record symptoms alongside what was eaten for a couple of weeks.\n\nIf your symptoms are severe, getting worse, or new and unexplained, please talk to a doctor. The guideline passage lists features that change how reflux is assessed (*GERD: empiric initial management and the alarm features that change it*). I can't assess your situation from here.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
